module gunbc.plans.demand_engine_program

import std.dissolution { unbound_dissolution }
import gunbc.plan { Plan, PlanRetiresWhen, PlanProjectsCommittedMarkdown }
import std.markdown { MarkdownBlock, TableBlock, AlignNone }
import gunbc.plans.md_helpers { h2, h3, p, li, ul, ol, cell, row, quote }

fn demand_engine_program_body() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    quote(blocks: [
      p(text: "**Status: decisions D1-D5 ruled; D6-D10 deferred with named triggers (2026-09-15, session fierce-seal-607).** No implementation lands from this document. It is the single design authority for one program: running ordinary sequential `.dag` programs at the loosest lawful structure their facts allow -- derived demand, one demand engine, lawful decomposition, and realization/materialization chosen outside program semantics -- without any program authoring dependency rows, widths or a private scheduler. M0 is additive authority establishment, not a replacement migration. Each later milestone that retires a production root is a replacement migration (DESIGN section 3 standing rule): that root is deleted in the change that lands its replacement, with a named production consumer."),
      p(text: "**Operator direction carried (2026-09-14/15):** concurrency comes through dependency structure, demand-driven like Bazel's execution phase; one shared ready queue drained by leased capacity -- no widths, waves, batches or shard plans; every existing width/concurrency mechanism is deleted, not frozen or kept behind a serial fallback; future programs inherit this without knowing DependencyView or DependencyRelation; realization is how things happen and materialization is how things are presented/stored; key definitions and hashing are a conformance concern; local processes are fabric supply bounded by an observed offer (in CI, the runner's caps); remote execution is a later strategy over the same graph and keys; no half migrations."),
      p(text: "**Third-party review folded in (side chat, 2026-09-15):** the universal unit is a semantic demand over a node and its context, not a raw node; demand identity and evaluation identity are two keys, and result content identity is a third joined by EvaluationResultBinding; semantic result contracts are not derived from cost; algebraic laws must be established under an observation contract before they license decomposition; occurrence identity is genuine identity at occurrence grain; dynamic dependencies across effects need a chosen protocol; the order below names its first authority cut. Section 7 records D1-D8 as ruled or deferred-with-trigger."),
      p(text: "**Operator rulings folded in (2026-09-15, second round):** D1 is ruled -- eval is the semantic judgment. Demand derivation is a deterministic authority; demand minimization is a deterministic authority; alternate implementations of either must be extensionally equivalent handlers of that one authority, and policy never chooses a different semantic demand population. Materialization is an interface with admissible handlers; realization is an interface with admissible handlers; policy selects ONLY among those admissible handlers, so trying another strategy is binding another handler, never a second eval; a realization may refine a judgment into internal work, regroup it under applicable law evidence and choose order, placement and transport, but it cannot change the semantic judgment population. The first production realization this program selects is emitted Rust; the current interpreter remains the production semantic handler until that route implements the complete judgment and refusal contract, after which the interpreter becomes an offline differential oracle, and another handler may later replace it without changing eval. The demand engine sees graph execution only: it is opaque to effect payloads -- it never interprets a shell invocation, a file write or a request -- and consumes only generic prerequisite relations and generic share/admission verdicts that effect analysis projects from declarations one layer below it. The demand graph is planned at compile time as far as the language allows: every demand template, relation constructor, conditional alternative and typed membership slot is derived by the compiler; runtime never invents a dependency kind. `Branch`, `Match` and `Loop` are static demands with a runtime selector; a host call is a demand at its call site whose result is an ordinary value, opaque inside; the members of a collection that arrives from outside instantiate concrete demands only through their declared template. Laws are typed evidence consumed only by decomposition, established at an honestly reported rung and applied only within their carried bound."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "1. Goal and displaced cost"),
    p(text: "**Goal.** An author writes ordinary, sequential-looking `.dag`. The compiler derives the semantic demands the program makes and their dependencies from program structure, effect contracts, external-input observations and established algebraic laws. A demand engine evaluates only what requested targets transitively need, releases dependents the moment a required result is available, produces each equal share-admitted demand once, serves repeated demands from a store, and places heavy work on leased local processes (later, remote executors). None of this is authored per feature."),
    p(text: "**First full-corpus reading (operator run, 2026-09-15, main `1d5c804fe6a`, instrument: the native fold's `[native-cost-partition]` line, verdict NativeDriverCostReconciled).** The bounded-slice framing this document inherited -- per-module prepare versus eval -- was pre-registered against the full fold and FALSIFIED: on the full corpus prepare and eval together are under 2% of the driver span. What dominates is `context`, the assemble-once corpus ingest (scanned_paths 5855, ingested_paths 2360 to evaluate a few hundred identities), at roughly 85% of the span; `receipt_admission` is the next row at roughly 10%, three orders of magnitude above the evaluation it admits; and the emit-plus-build of the native compiler, whose closure fingerprint was identical to the previous run's, sits OUTSIDE the partition at about half the process wall. What follows from it is separated into findings and ruled work (side-chat ruling, 2026-09-15). RULED: the first cross-run materialization family for T2b is `NativeCompilerArtifactRequest` (an IdempotentEffect after M1's contract) consuming the COMPLETE `PreMaterializationIdentity` -- producer compiler, source closure, target model, toolchain, build configuration including the `RustCratePartitionIdentity` of D9, required lens contract -- with the lookup placed BEFORE the work it avoids: a hit on a closure identity derivable from the canonical `.dag` closure bypasses both emission and the cargo build, and the receipt states which boundary it bypassed rather than claiming the whole outer span; discriminator: same complete identity -> verified hit, emitted-source writer not entered, cargo not entered; changed producer/toolchain/partition/lens contract -> miss, emit and build. NEXT DERIVATION, not yet ruled: context assembly is the first engine-cut candidate -- the engine needs an authority cheaper than full ingestion (a source-root manifest, per-file parse or module-summary judgments, the module dependency closure of the requested targets) so that undemanded modules are not parsed, normalized, resolved or loaded, which is a vertical stage cut of M6's kind moved forward into M3a; enumeration may remain. Context identity is NOT tree identity alone: it depends on the requested target population, the sealed read set, tree identity, compiler and evaluator semantics identity, grammar identity, ingestion policy, external observations and the occurrence contract, and the recommended reuse shape is occurrence-neutral per-file/module artifacts from which a graph-local context is assembled -- a separate materialization cut, never hidden inside the engine cut because both attack one timing row. M3a is amended only after the exact context producer, child judgment grain, displaced loop and bypass discriminator are named. REQUIRED CHAIN READ: `receipt_admission` -- subpartition it (row decoding, identity joins, population reconciliation, canonicalization, completeness checks, cost reconciliation, rendering), pre-register which producer multiplicities should collapse, and move a repeated full-population join to a qualified carrier only on that reading; no receipt cache and no weakened admission from the single figure. REQUIRED PRE-ANCHOR CENSUS: every refusal attributed and contract-classified at identity grain (subject, requested judgment, v2 outcome, attributed cause, contract disposition, v1 outcome as differential evidence only, owner, remedy or declared capability boundary), with the terminal populations named -- accepted where the contract requires refusal; refused where the contract requires acceptance; typed refusal permitted by the contract; unattributed refusal; result differing from the complete result contract -- the unattributed population reaching zero before any anchor (D10). Re-derive the numbers from that run's persisted rows and partition line, not from this paragraph."),
    h2(text: "2. The model (terminal architecture)"),
    h3(text: "2.1 Semantics, demand, minimization, materialization, realization, policy"),
    p(text: "DESIGN section 3 keeps interface, realization and policy as three facts, and section 2 minimizes the demand graph before materializing answers. The run of a program is therefore composed, not identified with its execution strategy:"),
    TableBlock {
      header: row(cells: [cell(text: "Layer"), cell(text: "Owns"), cell(text: "Never owns")]),
      alignments: [AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone],
      rows: [
        row(cells: [cell(text: "Semantics (eval)"), cell(text: "what judgment a program denotes, sequential as authored"), cell(text: "order, concurrency, placement, storage")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "Demand derivation"), cell(text: "deterministic authority: which judgments requested targets require"), cell(text: "how or where they are computed; policy selection")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "Demand minimization"), cell(text: "deterministic authority: one production serving several demands at their least common ancestor"), cell(text: "cost-based choices; policy selection")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "Materialization"), cell(text: "interface with admissible handlers: recompute, share, memoize, encoding, retention, store"), cell(text: "what outputs a judgment owes; the demand population")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "Realization"), cell(text: "interface with admissible handlers: order, concurrency, grouping, placement, transport"), cell(text: "semantic identity; the demand population")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "Policy"), cell(text: "selects ONLY among admissible materialization and realization handlers (admissibility, trust, resource fit, retention, cost ranking)"), cell(text: "semantics, derivation, minimization, or output obligations")]),
      ],
    },
    p(text: "In the corpus's existing wording (`v2.std.materialize`): `run ≜ realize ∘ materialize ∘ dependency_view`, with minimization before materialize and eval supplying each demanded judgment's meaning. The operator's framing -- realization is how things happen, materialization is how things are presented and stored -- is exactly the two right-hand layers; eval names the semantic judgment (D1, ruled 2026-09-15)."),
    h3(text: "2.2 The unit: a semantic demand, not a raw node"),
    p(text: "`Node` is the universal structural carrier; not every node is independently runnable, and one structural node answers differently under different environments, inferred facts, resolved bindings, interpretations, external observations and semantics versions. The unit of demand is a **judgment** over structure and context, keyed through the existing node structure without minting a second graph. `JudgmentContract` -- subject/input schema, result contract, demand nature, identity derivation, persistence projection -- is THE authority; `std.materialization_provider` `ArtifactRequest` is its persistence projection. A hand-maintained universal `JudgmentKind` coproduct beside `ArtifactRequest` is refused; a closed coproduct is admissible only as a derived projection of the contract population. Semantic demand grain, realization dispatch grain and materialization storage grain are separate facts: thousands of tiny demands may run in one in-process continuation, be fused into one child-process request, or be persisted as one aggregate artifact."),
    h3(text: "2.3 Identity: named keying relations, with evaluation split from result content"),
    p(text: "Following `docs/plans/keying-relation-design.md` (a key is a named relation within a named scope):"),
    TableBlock {
      header: row(cells: [cell(text: "Identity"), cell(text: "Denotes"), cell(text: "Scope"), cell(text: "Derived from"), cell(text: "Existing authority")]),
      alignments: [AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone],
      rows: [
        row(cells: [cell(text: "structural node identity"), cell(text: "what a node is"), cell(text: "global"), cell(text: "Merkle fold of kind, edge labels, child identities; occurrence excluded"), cell(text: "`v2.std.node` `content_hash`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "demand identity"), cell(text: "the question asked"), cell(text: "global"), cell(text: "judgment kind + structural subject + explicit arguments + static semantic context"), cell(text: "none general yet; `std.materialization_provider` `request_key` for today's families")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "dependency read set"), cell(text: "the exact judgments and observations an answer consumed"), cell(text: "one answer"), cell(text: "sealed when the demand reaches its terminal value"), cell(text: "none yet")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "evaluation identity"), cell(text: "the judgment under its exact consumed inputs"), cell(text: "global, location- and provider-free"), cell(text: "demand identity + sealed read-set identities + observation identities + evaluator/compiler semantics identity"), cell(text: "`std.materialization_provider` `request_key` / `artifact_request_key` (the input-derived key; analogue)")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "result content identity"), cell(text: "digest of what that evaluation produced"), cell(text: "global, content of the result"), cell(text: "canonical encoding of the produced outputs"), cell(text: "`std.materialization_provider` output-part digests (analogue)")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "occurrence identity"), cell(text: "this authored occurrence"), cell(text: "one allocator (one source graph)"), cell(text: "the graph-scoped allocator; never content, span, spelling or path"), cell(text: "`std.occurrence_identity` scope law; `ScopedOccurrenceRef` for cross-graph references")]),
      ],
    },
    p(text: "**EvaluationResultBinding** joins an evaluation identity to the result content identity it produced. Same evaluation identity with different content identities is a conflict/impurity, never convergence. Do not call the input-derived key \"this answer\"; that nickname mixed the judgment with its product."),
    p(text: "**Keys are algorithmic.** Each identity is a total derivation into a canonical encoding that is injective over its domain. A finite hash of that encoding is never injective. The hash family is chosen by sharing scope, and each scope declares a collision disposition. Structural FNV (`Fnv1a64Structural`) is a bucket/index only, never silent equality: the exact canonical preimage is compared before serving. A cross-run or store key uses an approved cryptographic family appropriate to the named relation and scope; a hit still verifies the canonical preimage before serving. Upstream-mandated SHA-1 (git) is admitted only as THAT upstream relation's identity with its explicit collision disposition -- it is not a general cross-run family. Live migration named here: `std.materialization_provider` `request_key` is FNV-structured while its requests already cross persistent-store boundaries; M1 migrates that route to an admitted cryptographic family, or records an explicit preimage-verified exception for that named relation until the migration lands. Hand-authored, positional or lossy encodings are defects; C1 is the recorded specimen -- a live cross-parse memo identity whose digest used the wrong unary byte domain. The `conformance-identity` domain owns the key RELATION (what relation, what scope, what complete canonical preimage derives it, what equality and collision disposition govern it); identity owns whether the declared inputs are complete. `conformance-realization` owns whether a handler, provider or placement faithfully realizes an admitted identity and result contract (completeness, readback, retention, transport, refusal, binding obligations) -- whether the handler consumes the admitted key without substituting, truncating or bypassing it. Cache purity is one discriminator across that boundary, not the whole realization domain."),
    p(text: "**Occurrence identity is not a computation key and is not demoted.** It distinguishes structurally equal occurrences (the two `x` in `x + x`) for binding, diagnostics and provenance. A result that depends only on occurrence-neutral structure is already reusable across graphs; a result containing or indexed by occurrence identities must declare one contract: semantic-only (persist occurrence-neutral values, rebind locally after load), scoped (persist `ScopedOccurrenceRef`s usable while their scope is addressable), or rebinding (persist structural anchors plus a proved mapping into the destination graph). A bare occurrence ordinal is never interpreted in another allocator."),
    h3(text: "2.4 Dependencies are derived; declarations of underivable facts stay"),
    p(text: "The engine's relation is `v2.std.dependency` `DependencyRelation` over demand identity; compiler `DependencyView`s project into it. Semantic dataflow and control flow come from the section 4 behaviors: a call demands callee and arguments; `Bind` orders later computation after its value; `Branch` and `Match` demand condition or scrutinee, then exactly one arm of a statically known set, chosen by that value -- a static demand with a runtime selector, never a dynamic edge; products demand components per construction; `Loop` demands its static body with a data-valued trip count under its descent evidence. A host call -- a shell invocation, a REST request -- is a demand at its call site and nothing more: its inputs are demanded, its result is an ordinary value, and the engine neither interprets nor expands what the call does inside. A list that arrives from such a call is consumed like any authored list: `map`, `fold` and per-item `Match` over it are static demands whose membership is data. The compiler therefore derives every demand template, relation constructor, conditional alternative and typed membership slot; runtime never invents a new dependency kind or an untyped relation. Selector values activate statically declared alternatives; runtime collection members and call arguments instantiate concrete demands and relations through their declared templates, and every instantiated subject is keyed, admitted, entered into the read set and scheduled by the one demand engine -- a realization handler that hid those child demands internally would be a second engine. Data edges carry operand bindings -- which producer output satisfies which consumer input. **Hand-authored rows that restate derivable dataflow or control flow are deleted** (today: `v2.program` `program_run_dependencies`). **Declarations of facts the language cannot derive stay**: effect declarations, host-call input/output contracts, idempotency and freshness contracts, external observation identities, explicit workflow barriers, resource and trust constraints, opaque implementation boundaries. The engine never interprets them. Effect and resource analysis projects their ordering consequences into the generic prerequisite relations `v2.std.dependency` already carries (`EffectDependsOn`, `ResourceDependsOn`, `BarrierBefore`, `PlacementDependsOn`), which the engine consumes exactly like data prerequisites -- so `write A; write B` with no data edge still orders B after A inside the one engine rather than in a private readiness graph in some handler -- and into generic share and materialization admissions (`std.materialization_ladder` already separates PureComputation, IdempotentEffect, WorldRead and FreshEffect; a fresh effect is neither attached in flight nor served from a prior result). Realization consumes the full effect and resource envelope to select placement and acquire grants; the host-effect boundary performs the operation (section 2.7). An undeclared or unresolved effect never reads as independence: an opaque host call without an established envelope couples conservatively, because not interpreting shell cannot mean treating shell as pure."),
    h3(text: "2.5 The demand engine"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Demand closure.** Evaluation starts from requested targets and pulls transitive demands; undemanded judgments never enter lookup, readiness or realization."),
      li(text: "**Static demand schemas, dynamically admitted instances.** A demand's templates and relation constructors are planned before it runs; no dependency kind is discovered at runtime. What runs decides selector values (which arm, how many iterations) and the members of declared collections (which files the shell call listed), each instantiating a concrete demand through a slot the plan already carries with a typed subject shape; the engine keys it, attaches it to any in-flight equal share-admitted demand, detects cycles, inserts its relations and records its read. A running demand whose selected arm or admitted member is not yet available suspends and resumes on availability; that is the whole dynamic protocol (D4). The exact read set -- which arms and members were actually consumed -- is sealed at the terminal value and only then yields the evaluation identity. One equal, share-admitted demand is produced once."),
      li(text: "**Availability, not scheduling.** Per demand: waiting, ready, lease pending, running, suspended, commit pending, available, refused, blocked by a prerequisite. Only available -- the exact result contract admitted -- releases dependents. A verdict bundle full of red tests is available; a crash or a corrupt object is not."),
      li(text: "**One live ready queue.** A demand whose prerequisites are available enters the one queue immediately; any completion immediately re-evaluates its direct dependents. No layers, batches or waves. Discriminating case (A1 -> B and an independent A2): B becomes READY immediately after A1 becomes available, without waiting for A2. Actual simultaneous execution of B with A2 is the first control of the local-child-process cut, not of the one-seat in-process engine."),
      li(text: "**One dispatch policy.** When ready work exceeds capacity: stable order by demand identity, admit the next demand whose requirements obtain a lease, leave unadmittable demands ready. No feature supplies another policy."),
      li(text: "**Typed blocked states.** Cycle, missing edge, refused prerequisite, waiting for capacity, running, suspended, lookup pending, no demand and key conflict are distinct."),
    ]),
    h3(text: "2.6 Lawful algebraic decomposition"),
    p(text: "A dependency relation alone leaves the commonest sequential shape -- a fold whose step reads the previous accumulator -- a chain. Established algebraic laws break chains: associativity with identity licenses regrouping; commutativity, order-free combination; idempotence, collapsing duplicates; a homomorphism, pushing a map through a split; independent product dimensions, concurrent components. Three conditions bound this:"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**A law is evidence, not a record shape.** Today `Monoid` and `CommutativeMonoid` in `std.algebra` are field-identical: inhabiting them establishes an operation and an identity value, not associativity. Decomposition needs named law evidence such as `AssociativeUnder<ObservationContract>`, `CommutativeUnder`, `IdempotentUnder` and `HomomorphismBetween`, established over the complete observable result -- value, diagnostic population and order, effect requests and order, provenance, refusal shape, termination. An unestablished law leaves the sequential dependence intact."),
      li(text: "**Associativity permits many trees; the canonical one is chosen.** Stable incremental reuse needs an append-stable decomposition (content-defined chunks under a Merkle hierarchy, or a persistent sequence with canonical branching) so that appending invalidates a bounded path by construction."),
      li(text: "**Granularity is priced by total cost**: semantic work plus dependency bookkeeping, queueing, encoding, lookup, lease acquisition, process startup, transfer, commit and reduction -- otherwise splitting wins on paper and loses in execution."),
    ]),
    p(text: "This consumes the reduce-spine and idle-lane direction of `docs/plans/machine-shape-orthogonal-scheduling.md` (computation graph x machine facts x algebraic evidence -> schedule) rather than restating it."),
    h3(text: "2.7 Realization: placement from fabric supply"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Placement** gains a truthful local child-process arm; current `std.realization` `Placement` has none, and `LocalInProcess` is not a child process."),
      li(text: "**Supply** is `product.fabric.supply` `SupplierOffer` over a local-process executor: one offer per conserved pool, quantity derived from the observed execution scope (effective cgroup CPU, memory and PID limits and usage) against the measured work footprint and named policy. An unobservable axis refuses; a footprint that does not fit yields zero, never one. In CI the runner slot is the scope."),
      li(text: "**Selection** goes through `product.fabric.selection` `CandidateOffer` and `select_supply`, with capacity-class admission, even with one offer. Selection is not occupancy."),
      li(text: "**Occupancy is two-level**: a host-global grant reserves each realizing run's scope (in CI the runner slot already is that grant); an in-parent atomic ledger subdivides it per demand by resource vector. No committed lease, no spawn; release on every terminal arm, only after quiescence. A `SupplierOffer` with no committed lease path is half-wired and is not an occupancy result."),
      li(text: "**Nested schedulers** (cargo, make) take tokens from the demand's committed claim and never choose their own width."),
      li(text: "**The crate structure of the emitted closure is a Rust realization over a derived compilation-unit interface, and it is a third lever beside scheduling and reuse.** The native route emits `src/v2/compiler/00_compile.dag`'s closure as ONE crate (`gunbc-emitted-closure-src-v2-compiler-00-compile-dag`, lib `v1_compiled`: 181 emitted modules, ~87k lines, six external dependencies; instrument: the native route's emit and build log). rustc's front half is single-threaded per crate, so on a many-core builder that crate's dominant phase runs on one core; cargo parallelizes across crates, and its crate-level scheduling is an existing realization handler doing what it already does -- so a crate DAG is emitted STRUCTURE, not an engine width, and lies outside the no-widths ruling. The homes already exist and this program reuses them, never a hand-authored crate roster (a second positional authority for the module graph): `v2.std.compilers.compilation_unit` owns separate-compilation units, their derived interfaces (referenced declarations and by-value type-definition closure) and unit dependencies; `v2.workflow.rust_crate_partition` owns Rust partition candidates, Rust admissibility (`validate_partition_r4`: `SccSplit`, `UnitGraphCycle`, `ByValueTypeCycle`) and the still-unimplemented policy resolver (`PolicyPartitionParam`, `PolicyResolverUnimplemented`) beside today's explicit interim partition; `std.decision` selects within an admitted, funded candidate field; `v1.emit_rust` and the native route RENDER the selected workspace -- `pub use`, external crate paths, manifests, a stable facade crate -- and never decide it. Source acyclicity (DESIGN §3) is necessary, not sufficient: candidates derive from the typed resolved-reference graph plus runtime and emitter edges, complete cross-unit interfaces and Rust admissibility. The textual `crate::` graph over emitted `.rs` is an INSTRUMENT, not an authority: its measured reading (181 modules, 1,331 edges, 179 SCCs, one 3-module SCC \{std_content_hash, std_occurrence_identity, v2_std_node\}) is not a source-import cycle -- the authored imports are acyclic -- so that SCC is an unresolved emitted-reference SCC to be classified by edge provenance (semantic source reference | derived cross-unit interface | runtime/prelude | reexport/facade | emitter-generated helper | lexical false positive) before it may enter the partition authority as an atom; an emitter-added reverse edge is an emitter defect, a genuine Rust-target requirement is a realization edge. `v1_rt` (fan-in 177 of 181, already alone in the stage0 `v1-stage0-runtime` crate) is a foundation-crate CANDIDATE and the implementation of a runtime-support contract; the obligation that survives self-hosting is that the emitted closure receives a runtime interface faithfully realizing every intrinsic its modules use, not that a module of that name exists. The native consumer keeps one stable root/facade surface; internal crate names are realization details. The selected partition enters generation identity as a canonical `RustCratePartitionIdentity` -- the module-to-unit assignment, the derived unit interfaces and dependencies, and the stable facade identity -- named as a component of `build_configuration` in `GenerationIdentity` / `PreMaterializationIdentity`, so two partitions of one closure are different build inputs, never same-evaluation/different-content impurity; the `SelectionReceipt` travels beside it as decision provenance and is not itself a build input, because two decisions under different evidence or policy may select the same partition."),
    ]),
    h3(text: "2.8 Materialization: share, recompute, memoize, store"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Share** within a run is demand minimization: one equal, share-admitted demand is produced once, and later requesters attach in flight. Equal pure demands share one production; fresh-effect invocations are distinct productions; world reads share only under an admitted observation/staleness identity; idempotent effects share or retry only under their declared idempotency contract."),
      li(text: "**Recompute** is an explicit decision below the materialization ladder's cost floor (`std.materialization_ladder`), not a default. Today the floor is an exempt roster; M4 replaces it with a measured serve-versus-recompute decision."),
      li(text: "**Memoize and store** under collision-resistant evaluation identity bound to a verified result content identity (`extdeps.realization.materialization_store_local` today). Lookup precedes capacity: a verified hit takes no lease and releases dependents; an unavailable or corrupt store refuses and never reads as a miss. A hit still verifies the canonical preimage; same evaluation identity with a different content identity refuses as impurity."),
      li(text: "**Cross-run hit protocol.** Two routes are admissible. Manifest-validation (first consumer): DemandIdentity looks up a MUTABLE evaluation-index record \{read-set manifest, EvaluationIdentity, ResultContentIdentity\}, validates that current member identities match the sealed read set, then serves the IMMUTABLE content-addressed result or takes a typed arm. Four arms with different owners: index absent is ordinary cold state; index stale is a validation miss -- re-evaluate and CAS a successor generation; index corrupt or unreadable is typed `EvaluationIndexCorrupt` / `EvaluationIndexUnreadable` with an observation/repair receipt and a named quarantine-delete-rebuild transition -- re-evaluation may FOLLOW that typed transition and is never the first and only observable outcome; immutable result absent is a materialization miss. The index is a mutable alias with compare-and-set/generation on update: two evaluations racing for the same DemandIdentity CAS the index; the loser re-reads and either attaches to the winner's verified binding or re-evaluates if the winner aborted. Same EvaluationIdentity with different ResultContentIdentity is a conflict, never a CAS winner. The immutable store stays content-addressed independently of the mutable alias. Discovery-first: re-run pure dependency discovery, form the exact read set, derive EvaluationIdentity, then immutable lookup. **Ruled for the first consumer: manifest-validation.** Discovery-first re-executes the dependency pass the demand engine already performs and remains an admissible later handler."),
      li(text: "**Semantic result contracts are interface, not cost.** `JudgmentContract` is the authority; `ArtifactRequest` is only its persistence projection, derived when persistence is selected -- contract first, then optional materialization, never cost first."),
      li(text: "**Encoding.** Results crossing a process or run boundary use a canonical, versioned encoding with decode refusal and identity readback; shared context is materialized results consumed by dependents, never an inherited process address space."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "3. Relationship to existing authorities and plans"),
    TableBlock {
      header: row(cells: [cell(text: "Authority / plan"), cell(text: "Relationship")]),
      alignments: [AlignNone, AlignNone],
      rows: [
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`v2.std.materialize` (`run ≜ realize ∘ materialize ∘ dependency_view`, analysis-side)"), cell(text: "**Consumed and made live.** Its structural identity grade and spine wording are this program's; M3a is its first runtime.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`docs/plans/keying-relation-design.md`"), cell(text: "**Consumed.** Its named-relation-in-scope model is section 2.3; M1 is its first large consumer.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`docs/plans/realization-measurement-loop.md`"), cell(text: "**Consumed.** Its gate (realize(T) content-addressed at minimal placement; uncached non-redundant work is an error) is M5's acceptance.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`docs/plans/machine-shape-orthogonal-scheduling.md`"), cell(text: "**Consumed.** Its reduce-spine, idle-lane and algebraic-evidence direction is section 2.6.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`docs/plans/resource-aware-scheduler.md`, `bounded-input-cost-envelope-scheduling` (authority-only)"), cell(text: "**Superseded on width.** `spawn_width` formulas are replaced by lease admission against an observed offer. Bounded-input admission and predicted-versus-measured cost roles are retained.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`docs/plans/serving-capacity-home-ruling.md`"), cell(text: "**Consistent.** Shared selection, subject-specific occupancy; local processes get their own producer and linearization.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`docs/plans/content-hash-family-grounding.md`"), cell(text: "**Consumed.** This program adds the family-by-sharing-scope rule.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`v2.lens.cost`, `std.realization_schedule` `CostAccount`"), cell(text: "**Consumed.** Symbolic cost and measured/predicted axes exist; M4 joins them to demand and realization identities.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`v2.workflow.scheduler` / `executor` / `runtime_run`, `module_resolution_plan`; `v2.workflow.realization_runner` scheduling surfaces"), cell(text: "**Split.** M3a cuts the native-fold scheduling loop. M3b migrates `v2.program` and `module_resolution_plan`, relocates `NodeKeyedGraphArtifact`, `NodeKeyedGraphRow`, `NodeKeyedGraphArtifactEntry`, `node_keyed_graph_row_lookup` / `NodeKeyedGraphRowMissing`, `node_keyed_graph_transitive_bytes` / `NodeKeyedGraphTransitiveBytesMissingRow`, and deletes unconsumed scheduler/batch/runner-schedule/`runtime_run` surfaces once the exact producer/consumer/disposition census reaches zero old scheduling consumers. The interpreter is not deleted.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`std.materialization_provider` `ArtifactRequest`"), cell(text: "**Persistence projection of `JudgmentContract`, not a parallel authority.** Generalized in M2 only as that projection.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`conformance-identity` / `conformance-realization`"), cell(text: "**Partition.** `conformance-identity` owns the key RELATION -- what relation, what scope, what complete canonical preimage derives it, what equality and collision disposition govern it -- and whether the declared inputs are complete. `conformance-realization` owns whether a handler, provider or placement faithfully realizes an admitted identity and result contract (completeness, readback, retention, transport, refusal, binding obligations) without substituting, truncating or bypassing the admitted key. Cache purity is one discriminator across that boundary, not the whole realization domain.")]),
      ],
    },
    h2(text: "4. Current census: what each root's deletion breaks"),
    p(text: "**DISCOVERY-ONLY roster of roots**, not a transcribed population. Re-derive dependents by file-mention grep of each root's symbols over production and test trees at the milestone's delete-first branch; do not copy hit counts into this document. Every cut still owes an exact producer/consumer/route/disposition census. The loud census is the fail-closed floor on that delete-first branch, run per milestone before its detailed plan."),
    TableBlock {
      header: row(cells: [cell(text: "Root"), cell(text: "Named production sites (symbols)")]),
      alignments: [AlignNone, AlignNone],
      rows: [
        row(cells: [cell(text: "scheduler layers, executor, realization_runner, runtime_run"), cell(text: "`v2.program`, `module_resolution_plan`, `operand_flow`, a plan row, `doc_graph_roots`, `resolved_graph_cache.rs`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "hand-authored DependencyView rows"), cell(text: "`v2.program` `program_run_dependencies` and fixture rows")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "v2.lens.parallelism (no independent arm; coupling evidence always unresolved)"), cell(text: "`v2.lens.parallelism`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "eval as a recursive child fold"), cell(text: "`00_compile` native driver, `v2.program`, `runtime_run`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "floor discovery width (`DiscoveryWidthPolicy`, `CONTROLLED_WIDTH`)"), cell(text: "required-floor discovery Rust")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`derived_realization_schedule.rs`"), cell(text: "`derived_realization_schedule.rs`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`std.realize_pack` advisory width"), cell(text: "`std.realize_pack`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`std.realization_width` fallback / minimum-one"), cell(text: "`std.realization_width`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "parse sweep unbounded threads"), cell(text: "`claim_executor`, `declaration_index`, `module_path_index`, `namespace_wave_admission`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "build parallelism (`ci_compile_jobs`, jobserver, `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS`)"), cell(text: "`ci_compile_jobs`, jobserver, `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "results indexed by occurrence identity crossing graphs"), cell(text: "allocator, minted ids, span index")]),
      ],
    },
    h2(text: "5. Milestones"),
    p(text: "Order: M0 -> M1 -> M2 -> M3a -> M3b -> M4 -> M5 -> M6 -> M7. The first authority cut is M3a (one-seat in-process); remaining scheduler deletion is M3b. Law-bearing decomposition is last: do not optimize stage-wide folds that M6 deletes. Each lists what it makes true, what it deletes in the same change, its production consumer, its qualification and its exit."),
    h3(text: "M0 — Identity is a conformance domain; this program is the authority"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** `gunbc.design_argument` `conformance_domains` gains `conformance-identity` (keys and hashing); `gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_review_criteria` derives its reviewer; this plan is registered and projected."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** Nothing (additive authority)."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** The derived review criterion, read on every change."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Every home resolves; the reviewer roster and the generated DESIGN.md include the domain (`roadmap_review_role_witness_test` `production_conformance_domains_appear_in_generated_design`, `roadmap_review_function_witness_test` reviewer list)."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** Operator decisions D1-D10 recorded in this document, and side-chat sign-off."),
    h3(text: "M1 — Semantic demand and identity contract"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** `JudgmentContract` as the judgment authority (subject/input schema, result contract, demand nature, identity derivation, persistence projection); demand identity, dependency read set, evaluation identity, result content identity and EvaluationResultBinding as named keying relations with injective canonical encodings, family-by-scope hashing and a declared collision disposition per scope; per-judgment result contracts classifying semantic value, diagnostics, provenance, effects and refusal, with each occurrence-bearing output's contract (semantic-only, scoped or rebinding); evaluator and compiler semantics identity; external observation identity. Existing keys (`content_hash`, `request_key`, `typed_module_key`, the parse-table subject, store keys) inhabit the relations. `ArtifactRequest` remains only the persistence projection. `std.materialization_provider` `request_key` migrates off FNV-as-store-key onto an admitted cryptographic family, or is recorded as an explicit preimage-verified exception for that named relation."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** A second derivation of the same named relation in the same declared scope; lossy digest arms; any enrolled production key read across a scope its relation does not declare; any hand-maintained universal `JudgmentKind` coproduct standing beside `ArtifactRequest`."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** The native fold's existing `ArtifactRequest` families re-expressed as `JudgmentContract` persistence projections, keyed through the relations (no scheduling yet)."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Per relation: an injectivity witness over boundary cases of the canonical encoding with a collapsing mutation that reds; a family-by-scope refusal of a structural fingerprint as a cross-run key; a witness that a fingerprint collision is caught by content verification rather than served; a witness that two parses of one program with different occurrence ids yield equal demand and evaluation identities while their occurrence-scoped outputs refuse cross-scope interpretation."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** Every enrolled production key in the native-fold judgment population inhabits a named relation in its declared scope."),
    h3(text: "M2 — Derived dependencies and the dynamic-demand protocol"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** Static dataflow and control-flow dependencies derived from section 4 behaviors, with operand bindings; Branch/Match/Loop as static demands with runtime selectors; host-call sites as opaque demands whose collection results fill declared membership slots; suspension on an unavailable selector or member, member admission and read-set sealing (D4); `v2.lens.parallelism` answers independence from admitted relations. `ArtifactRequest` stays the persistence projection of `JudgmentContract`."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** `v2.program` `program_run_dependencies` and every fixture row that restates derivable dataflow."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** `v2.program`'s run and `module_resolution_plan`'s module dependencies, derived rather than written. Execution follows the adopted consumer-relative sequence (M2a: derive -> switch `v2.program` -> delete `program_run_dependencies`; M2b: one next consumer); `module_resolution_plan`'s future work is adapting its already-derived import authority to judgment dependencies and replacing its batch realization, not deleting a hand-authored population."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Derived relations equal the hand rows on every existing specimen before the rows are deleted; a missing-edge mutation makes admission refuse instead of reading as independence; an undeclared effect refuses concurrent realization at the realization layer, not in the engine; a selected arm's read set differs by branch and yields different evaluation identities."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** No production dependency relation restates derivable program flow."),
    h3(text: "M3a — Native-fold authority cut (one-seat in-process)"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** Section 2.5 with Share and explicit Recompute on the native fold: demand closure, suspension, availability states, one ready queue, one dispatch policy, typed blocked states, in-flight attachment, one-seat in-process realization. The native fold's driver becomes a `.dag` demand over its universe; the emitted main reduces to the host-effect boundary. M3a deletes the native fold's SCHEDULING loop only. The current interpreter remains the production semantic handler until emitted Rust implements the complete judgment and refusal contract."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** The Rust module loop in `v1.compiler.emit_rust` `emit_source_root_eval_driver_main_rs`. The interpreter is not deleted and is not demoted to an oracle at this cut."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** The v2 native fold (landing receipts) -- the first production root that switches to the engine and loses its manual scheduling loop."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Byte-identical driver rows against the pre-cutover fold on the bounded concurrent A/B harness and one capped exact-head full fold; undemanded judgments never run; B becomes READY immediately after A1 becomes available, without waiting for A2 (mutation delaying A1's availability keeps B waiting); equal share-admitted pure demands yield one production and multiple attachments (mutation produces twice); fresh-effect invocations remain distinct productions; world reads share only under an admitted observation/staleness identity; idempotent effects share or retry only under their declared idempotency contract; a refused prerequisite never unblocks; each typed blocked state reachable."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** No native-fold production program run outside the engine; the interpreter still implements the live judgment until the emitted-Rust completeness trigger."),
    h3(text: "M3b — Remaining scheduler migration and deletion"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** `v2.program` and `module_resolution_plan` run on the engine. Named graph-artifact authorities relocate: `NodeKeyedGraphArtifact`, `NodeKeyedGraphRow`, `NodeKeyedGraphArtifactEntry`, intern/lookup rows, `node_keyed_graph_transitive_bytes` and the missing-row refusal arms (`NodeKeyedGraphRowMissing`, `NodeKeyedGraphTransitiveBytesMissingRow`)."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** Unconsumed `v2.workflow.scheduler` layer planning, `v2.workflow.executor` batches, `v2.workflow.realization_runner` scheduling surfaces (`runner_dispatch_job`, `runner_dispatch_jobs`, `runner_dispatch_batch`, `runner_walk_batches`, `RunnerSchedulePlan`, `runner_schedule_plan_from_dependencies`, `runner_schedule_store_covers_nodes`, `runner_schedule_batch_count`, `runner_schedule_contains_batch`, `runner_execution_dependencies` as a scheduler wrap), and `v2.workflow.runtime_run`'s sequential fold -- after an exact producer/consumer/disposition census reaches zero old scheduling consumers."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** `v2.program` and `module_resolution_plan` (and their mirrored host realization), once M3a holds the native fold."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Census of old scheduling consumers is empty; relocated graph-artifact authorities still refuse missing refs and still derive transitive bytes; no remaining production walk of antichain batches."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** No layer or batch executor remains as a production consumer."),
    h3(text: "M4 — Measured demand economics"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** Receipts join to demand and realization identities: semantic work, realization overhead, encoding and lookup, expected reuse, retention and resource footprint, as `std.realization_schedule` `CostAccount` axes per demand class; the ladder's exempt roster is replaced by a measured serve-versus-recompute decision."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** `cost_floor_exempt` roster entries replaced by measured decisions."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** The M3a engine's materialization choice for the native fold."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** A planted demand whose dispatch overhead exceeds its work is not split. Economics are conditional on admitted handlers: if a qualifying memoize/store provider exists, economics may select memoize/store; if memoization is economically required but no admitted provider exists, the choice is typed `MemoizeRequiredButNoAdmittedProvider`; if only recompute and share handlers exist, choose among those. A cost receipt missing its realization identity refuses admission to the decision. Economic preference is not realization capability."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** Materialization and grouping choices on the native fold are measurement-derived."),
    h3(text: "M5 — Realization and materialization selection; widths deleted (consumer-relative cuts)"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** Section 2.7 and 2.8 as five consumer-relative replacement migrations, each its own root deletion with supply AND occupancy (a `SupplierOffer` with no committed lease path is half-wired): (a) native local-child-process realization -- offer + committed lease + handler; deletes that route's old concurrency control; (b) persistent materialization for one native family -- canonical encoding, section 2.8 manifest-validation protocol, content verification; deletes that family's old store path; (c) required-floor discovery/parse sweep as engine demands; deletes `CONTROLLED_WIDTH`, `DiscoveryWidthPolicy`, and the unbounded thread scope for that consumer; (d) cargo/make/jobserver tokens from the committed claim; deletes independent `-j` and serial fallback; (e) residual width census; deletes the last fallback/minimum-one helpers when unconsumed. Also the realization-measurement-loop gate (uncached non-redundant work is a typed error)."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** Per cut above: the named old concurrency, store, width-policy, independent jobserver, and residual helper roots -- never a single simultaneous dump of every width integer before its consumer has a lease path."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** (a)-(b) the native fold; (c) the required floor; (d) cargo/make under that fold and floor; (e) the residual census of retired width helpers."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Observation refusal (missing CPU, memory or PID evidence yields no offer); subunit refusal; conservation (running claims never exceed the grant); two-run conservation on one host; release on spawn failure, refusal, timeout and signal; canonical output under shuffled completion; a verified hit takes no lease; store unavailable refuses; same EvaluationIdentity and same ResultContentIdentity concurrent commit converges on the prior commit after readback verification; same EvaluationIdentity and different ResultContentIdentity is typed impurity/conflict and neither result silently wins; an M-versus-T receipt recomputes only changed evaluation identities (counted); a repository census finds no production consumer of a retired width mechanism; byte-identical rows at every cardinality the offer yields."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** No feature-authored concurrency width, environment knob, shard count, minimum-one fallback, or refusal-to-serial path remains; concurrent cardinality derives only from admitted resource claims and active leases; every concurrent realization holds a committed lease; cross-run reuse on the landing-receipt path."),
    h3(text: "M6 — Compiler stages as vertical judgment cuts"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** One complete path at a time -- source demand, parse result, resolution result, inference and eval result, native verdict -- moved onto judgment-shaped demands, its stage-wide fold root deleted, then widened to the next path."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** Each stage-wide fold as its judgment form lands."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** The native fold, path by path."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Byte-identical results per cut; a one-file change recomputes only its dependents (counted); each cut's deleted root has no remaining production consumer."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** On an admitted warm-materialization path, recomputation is limited to changed evaluation identities and their declared transitive dependents, plus explicitly classified fresh/global obligations."),
    h3(text: "M7 — Law-bearing algebraic decomposition"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** Law evidence carriers under observation contracts, their establishment by inhabitance and witnesses, the canonical append-stable decomposition, and realization applying decomposition only where laws are established and economics favor it. Lands after M6 so it does not optimize stage-wide folds that M6 deletes."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** Any convention-level parallelism assumption about remaining sequential combines (none may be introduced before this milestone)."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** Remaining sequential combines on the M6 cut paths whose combine carries established evidence -- not the stage-wide folds M6 deletes."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Decomposed and sequential results identical over the full observation contract, including diagnostic order; an unestablished-law mutation stays sequential; an observation-changing combine (diagnostic order) is refused decomposition; appending one element recomputes a bounded path (counted)."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** Every production decomposition cites its law evidence."),
    h2(text: "6. Program-wide qualification"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Identity of results.** Every engine milestone is byte-identical in driver rows against its predecessor on the bounded concurrent A/B harness, then on one capped exact-head full fold; a difference stops the line."),
      li(text: "**Production-route pairing.** Every hand-specimen or supplied-boundary witness is paired with at least one production-route inhabitance witness. The real producer's value must reach the real consumer through the authority under test, and deleting or bypassing that integration must make the paired control red. Shape agreement alone does not discharge this obligation."),
      li(text: "**Full JudgmentContract equality.** Semantic-equivalence receipts compare the complete `JudgmentContract` result: value, ordered diagnostics, refusal arm and cause, provenance/occurrence contract, effect requests and receipts, and required artifact outputs. Native driver-row byte identity is an additional projection-specific requirement, never the complete generic equality criterion."),
      li(text: "**Discriminating controls.** Every new witness names its mutation and why it reds, and fits the floor's per-identity step budget (hand specimens, not the corpus)."),
      li(text: "**Deletion is the census.** Each milestone runs a delete-first branch through the fail-closed floor before its detailed plan, and lands with a census showing no production consumer of what it retired."),
      li(text: "**Conformance.** Every change is read by `conformance-identity`, `conformance-realization`, `conformance-compute` and `conformance-decision`; departures state reasons. `conformance-identity` owns the key relation and input completeness; `conformance-realization` owns faithful realization of an admitted identity and result contract. Cache purity is one discriminator across that boundary."),
      li(text: "**Cost is reported, not asserted.** Receipts name the instrument that re-derives them, never transcribed numbers."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "7. Operator decisions required before M1"),
    p(text: "Every decision row is Ruled or Deferred with a trigger. Do not transcribe a count beside the rows; the grep receipt lives in the PR body."),
    ol(items: [
      li(text: "**D1 — What does eval name?** Option A: eval is the semantic judgment and the run engine composes demand, minimization, materialization and realization (DESIGN-consistent as written; the side chat's recommendation). Option B: eval names the whole run (operator's 2026-09-15 framing), which requires restating DESIGN section 3's three-facts wording so semantics remains a separate authority. Recommendation: A, keeping the operator's realization/materialization split verbatim as the two outer layers. **Ruled 2026-09-15: A.** Eval is not pluggable. Demand derivation is a deterministic authority; demand minimization is a deterministic authority; alternative implementations of either may be extensionally equivalent handlers of that one authority, but policy cannot choose a different semantic demand population. Materialization is an interface with admissible handlers; realization is an interface with admissible handlers; policy selects ONLY among those admissible materialization and realization handlers. A strategy that shapes work from graph facts (cost, law evidence, size) is a realization or materialization handler that may regroup, order, place and retain but never changes which judgments are demanded. The production realization this program selects is emitted Rust; the current interpreter remains the production semantic handler until that route implements the complete judgment and refusal contract, after which the interpreter is the offline differential oracle and not a fallback."),
      li(text: "**D2 — The universal demand shape.** `JudgmentContract` keyed through existing node structure, or another equivalent that mints no second AST graph. Recommendation: `JudgmentContract` as the authority, with `ArtifactRequest` only its persistence projection. **Ruled 2026-09-15:** semantic demand and `product.fabric.demand` `Demand<P>` stay distinct carriers with no common parent; a semantic demand placed out of process produces Work and a fabric demand through a named realization projection, not inheritance, and the grain is not one-to-one (in-process realization and materialization hits produce none; retries produce a new fabric attempt for one semantic demand). A hand-maintained universal `JudgmentKind` coproduct beside `ArtifactRequest` is refused; a closed coproduct is admissible only as a derived projection of the contract population."),
      li(text: "**D3 — Key vocabulary.** Adopt section 2.3 identities (structural node, demand, read set, evaluation, result content, occurrence scope) plus EvaluationResultBinding, with collision-resistant families for cross-run evaluation identity and declared collision disposition per scope. Recommendation: adopt the split. **Ruled 2026-09-15: adopted**, including the evaluation / result-content split: same evaluation identity with different content identities is impurity, never convergence."),
      li(text: "**D4 — Runtime facts in a planned graph.** Static demand schemas, dynamically admitted instances. The compiler derives every template, alternative and typed membership slot; runtime invents no dependency kind. `Branch`, `Match` and `Loop` are static demands with a runtime selector; a host call is a demand at its call site whose result is a value, opaque inside; members of a collection that arrives from outside instantiate concrete demands through their declared template and are admitted by the one engine. The engine is opaque to effect payloads and consumes only the generic prerequisite relations and share admissions projected from declarations. The only runtime protocol is suspension on an unavailable selector or member. What remains one layer down, at the host-effect boundary in realization: how a demand that performed an effect and then suspends on a member is resumed. Options: resume in place (a continuation in the emitted program), replay under a declared idempotency contract, or refuse the shape with a typed diagnostic. Recommendation: refuse the shape until resume-in-place exists in emitted Rust; replay only under a declared idempotency contract; restart across a performed effect is never admitted. **Ruled 2026-09-15:** the schema/admission protocol above; refuse-until-resume at the host-effect boundary. Admission is a capability join -- the judgment's effect contract with the selected realization's continuation capability -- decided before the shape starts executing; the engine receives only the resulting verdict."),
      li(text: "**D5 — Law authority.** A law is a typed evidence value (`AssociativeUnder<Obs>`, `CommutativeUnder<Obs>`, `IdempotentUnder<Obs>`, `HomomorphismBetween`) that only the decomposition rule consumes; semantics never depends on one, and an absent law leaves the chain sequential -- a speed loss, never a correctness loss. Each is established at an honestly reported rung (DESIGN section 4b): a witness over a discriminating corpus with a planted non-associative red establishes it on the tested inputs (rung 2, the route for user-declared operations); an exhaustive witness over a small finite carrier (rung 3); structural derivation from lawful parts -- product of monoids, pointwise lift, max and min over a total order, set union (rung 3-4, the route for std composites); or citation of the modeled framework for the standard structures (rung 3). A law-evidence value carries its operation, domain, observation contract, evidence rung and applicability bound: a sampled witness authorizes decomposition only within its carried bound (the tested corpus or a range it structurally contains); exhaustive finite evidence requires a total completeness authority and, for associativity, every triple; structural derivation consumes law evidence for every premise behind a construction wall, so the record is not publicly forgeable; a citation establishes the abstract law and needs a separate inhabitance route showing the live operator is that operation under this representation. When evidence is absent or does not cover the exact subject, execution stays sequential. The observation contract -- value only, or value plus diagnostic population and order, effect requests and order, provenance, refusal shape -- is owned by the judgment's result interface, never selected by a consumer: a consumer may demand a distinct value-only projection but cannot narrow the producer's contract by ignoring axes it still returns. Recommendation: land the evidence types and the refuse-without-evidence rule in `std.algebra` at M7, witnesses as the first producers, structural derivation for std composites. **Ruled 2026-09-15:** that authority and landing milestone."),
      li(text: "**D6 — Canonical decomposition.** Content-defined chunks under a Merkle hierarchy, or a persistent sequence with canonical branching. Recommendation: decide in M7 with measurements; the requirement (bounded invalidation on append) is fixed now. **Deferred:** trigger is M7 measurements choosing the tree; the requirement is not reopened."),
      li(text: "**D7 — Host-global grant home** on hosts running several realizing runs: reuse runner-slot allocation or fabric cell reservation, or model a host ledger. Recommendation: reuse, decided by M5's census. **Deferred:** trigger is M5's occupancy census naming the grant home."),
      li(text: "**D8 — First authority cut.** The v2 native fold's driver (M3a). Recommendation: confirm. **Deferred:** trigger is M3a's detailed plan confirming that driver as the first production scheduling cut; the consumer is not changed here."),
      li(text: "**D9 — Crate partition of the emitted closure; generator promotion is a separate fact.** Two facts joined through generation identity, not one decision (side-chat ruling, 2026-09-15). GENERATOR PROMOTION -- seed (v1 emitting v2) versus self-hosted (v2 emitting itself) -- is an admission root cut governed by DESIGN §7 and D1: the seed remains the production generator until the self-hosted route satisfies the complete judgment and refusal contract, then the root cuts over; cost never keeps the seed as a semantic fallback, and it is not a `std.decision` candidate. PARTITION SELECTION is a realization selection over the closure the ACTIVE generator produced: hard constraints first (every module owned exactly once; every source and reference edge represented; complete `CompilationUnit` interface closure; Rust R4 validity; no illegal private access or impl ownership; the stable facade; complete semantic and refusal equivalence), then `std.decision` `select_realization` over a bounded declared candidate field -- one crate (the baseline, which remains a candidate), runtime + remainder, a few derived target-count cuts via `PartitionPolicy`, the policy-derived cut, SCC-per-unit as the diagnostic extreme -- funded on clean emitted-closure build wall, warm incremental wall over a declared edit population (a leaf, a high-fan-in foundation module, a large ordinary compiler module), total CPU, peak memory and process count, link/metadata/process overhead, artifact and binary size, and emission/workspace-construction wall; scoped by closure identity, producer compiler, toolchain, build profile, machine shape, candidate-field identity, evidence snapshot and policy; result `SelectedWithin`, never 'optimal'. At self-host cutover the same authority and decision re-run over the new closure; the prior selection is evidence, not an inherited answer. MEASUREMENT: the reading taken so far is a route wall partition -- seed workspace build 9m12s, closure emission ~5m, one-crate emitted build ~3m20s on srv1 -- not a rustc phase partition; `[native-prepare-split]` measures native-driver prepare, not rustc, and is not this instrument. The trigger is therefore the direct candidate A/B: each admitted candidate built from the same emitted closure, toolchain, profile, features and warm shared dependency target, recording per-crate start/end wall, ready/running crate concurrency, total wall and CPU, peak RSS and process count, link wall and artifact size; then the three edit classes with the crate DAG's PREDICTED rebuilt set pre-registered against cargo's observed rebuilt set. Pre-registered structural consequences if a derived cut is right: more than one admitted crate is ready during a nontrivial part of the build; cargo overlaps their compilation; observed rebuilt crates equal the predicted downstream closure after each edit; semantic outputs and refusals identical; the selected candidate wins under the declared field. Line share is not a bound on front-half time and is not a prediction. SEQUENCE: this is a standalone native-route realization cut with a current production consumer (the emitted native closure build) and a current displaced route (the one-crate writer), depending on no engine, fabric supply, cross-run store or width deletion -- it does NOT wait for M5. Its serial position is T1 -> D9 -> T2a -> T2b: it precedes T2b because the selected `RustCratePartitionIdentity` is part of the compiler artifact's build configuration and therefore of its evaluation identity from the first persistent implementation; if the candidate field selects the one-crate baseline, D9 records that `SelectedWithin` result, performs no physical cut, and the sequence continues; if the runner envelope cannot honestly bound multi-crate cargo concurrency, the authority and A/B still complete and execution defers to M5 -- no `-j` setting belongs here. **Deferred:** trigger = the edge-provenance classification of the emitted reference graph and the candidate A/B above; until both exist this row admits no implementation."),
      li(text: "**D10 — Native production admission: when the v2-compiled route becomes the production semantic route.** Operator direction (2026-09-15): anchor on the native route now and root-cause disagreements over time; v1 is not the semantic oracle -- the authority is the modeled `JudgmentContract`, and a v1/v2 disagreement is evidence demanding investigation, with neither side winning by age. Two cuts stay separate: native v2 EXECUTION becoming the production semantic route (this row) and the self-hosted GENERATOR replacing the seed (D9 / §7, which still requires the complete generation judgment and refusal contract; a native evaluator may be production while the seed still generates it). Admission is a typed whole-route verdict, `NativeProductionAdmission = NativeProductionAdmitted { contract_identity, required_subject_denominator, result_contract_coverage, refusal_census, divergence_dispositions } | NativeProductionRefused { causes }`, requiring: a named denominator of the judgments and subjects the route promises to serve; total outcomes -- every demanded subject yields an admitted result or an attributed typed refusal; zero unattributed refusals; no contract-violating acceptance; no contract-violating refusal inside the required denominator; every v1/v2 divergence classified against the independent contract or source authority; and no interpreter fallback after the cut. A v2 refusal where v1 accepted is worked after cutover only when the refusal is correct under the contract or the subject lies outside the declared denominator with that boundary explicit -- deferring a false refusal of a valid required subject is not a complete production handler. After cutover, a red parity or admission result latches `NativeProductionRevertRequired` or stops new admissions and awaits an explicit operator disposition; it never silently re-executes the judgment through v1 (the current routing consumer's fall-back-to-interpreted arm is exactly the escape hatch this row deletes). Recommendation: adopt, and run the pre-anchor census of §1 as its first instrument. **Deferred:** trigger is that census reaching zero unattributed refusals with every divergence dispositioned, at which point the admission verdict is computed for the declared denominator; the interpreter-remains-production sentence in M3a is retired by this row's `NativeProductionAdmitted` and by nothing else."),
    ]),
  ]
}

data demand_engine_program_plan: Plan = Plan {
  projection: PlanProjectsCommittedMarkdown,
  slug: "demand-engine-program",
  title: "Demand-engine program: derived demand, lawful decomposition, realization and materialization",
  body: demand_engine_program_body(),
  retirement: PlanRetiresWhen { condition: unbound_dissolution(description: "Delete this document when M7 exit holds and every earlier milestone's deletions are census-verified: program runs go through the demand engine, enrolled native-fold production keys inhabit named relations, no production dependency relation restates derivable program flow, no feature-authored concurrency width, environment knob, shard count, minimum-one fallback or refusal-to-serial path remains (M5), the store serves cross-run repeats on the landing-receipt path (M5), on an admitted warm-materialization path recomputation is limited to changed evaluation identities and their declared transitive dependents plus explicitly classified fresh/global obligations (M6), and every production decomposition cites law evidence (M7) -- at which point the carriers state the design and this document is a second copy of it.") }
}
